---
/**
 * The cover for "Receipts, not reviews", drawing the argument of the post in four beats.
 *
 *  1. Work completes and a receipt is issued: the paper draws itself, its lines fill in, a tick
 *     closes the work, and the business signs it with the key its own instance holds — a flamingo
 *     seal travels from the business mark into the left slot and a signature writes itself there.
 *  2. The customer's side counter-signs the same paper, violet into the right slot. Two
 *     signatures, one document.
 *  3. Both sides then hold the same proof: two copies slide out from behind the receipt, and each
 *     carries BOTH seal dots — neither side could have made it alone. A palm bracket holds them.
 *  4. Later, two reviews, each sealed — a sun band, a waiting dot that breathes, a row of blocks
 *     for the commitment the network keeps — while one shared window fills between them. When it
 *     closes both open in the same frame, and two spokes say one trigger opened both: neither was
 *     written in reaction to the other. The business side reveals an outcome code rather than a
 *     rating, which is the post's third section.
 *
 * Beat four owns 6.2 s of the 13.8 s loop because it is the point of the post. Nothing here
 * claims a shipped feature — no status chip, no badge, no interface. Receipts do not exist yet
 * and the post says so; this is an explanation, drawn.
 *
 * Built the way HeroFlow.astro is built, that piece being the approved standard. The scene ships
 * as inline SVG rather than being built in JS, so the box is never empty and a throw leaves a
 * finished picture. That markup is the composed frame at t = 11.6 s and the live clock starts
 * there, so there is no mount flicker either way; reduced motion is the same machine stepped to
 * that frame, carrying all four beats at once. It stops when nobody is looking, and has no
 * max-width of its own — aspect-ratio holds the box open so nothing below the cover jumps.
 */
---

<figure
  class="sdr"
  role="img"
  aria-label="A receipt for completed work is signed by a business and counter-signed by the customer's side, both keep the same twice-signed copy, and their two reviews stay sealed until one shared window opens them at the same moment."
>
